    {
      "ordinal": 3,
      "depth": 0,
      "kind": "section",
      "number": "2",
      "title": "Definition",
      "body": "Bascinets with perforated visors are helmets with movable perforated visors. Such visors do not include Eye slots.\nThis type of visor is depicted in some rare manuscripts in Western Europe from the late 14th century and early 15th century. A handful of later designs showing this kind of visor still survive through the 15th century.\nThere is no consensus among armor experts where such manuscripts depict actual perforated visors or badly drawn regular helmets (hundskulls for example).\nThose helmets use a solid metal visor to protect the face, attached by 2 different system:\n● Attached on the forehead by a movable piece of metal, similar to the “spoon” from Klappvisor,\n● Attached to the dome with hinges at the temples, similar to Pivoting visored Bascinets.",
      "page_from": 3,
      "page_to": 3,
      "stable_key": "perforated-visor-bascinets:2",
      "parent_key": null,
      "review_status": "extracted",
